# /services/acpid — the ACPI service: it runs the firmware's AML.
#
# svcmgr launches acpid post-handover on every boot, as a provider.
# `provides` makes svcmgr create acpid's service endpoint, serve its RECV as
# bootstrap cap[0], and publish two SENDs on it:
#   * acpid.power:auth — POWER_AUTHORITY-tokened (1 << 63), for the sleep and
#     reset requests; seed-only, and pwrmgr is the only recipe seeding it.
#   * acpid.deny:deny  — a no-authority twin (sentinel token 1) that the
#     SLEEP/RESET gate rejects; svctest uses it to assert the deny path.
# `seed = devmgr.acpi` delivers cap[1], a SEND on devmgr's registry badged
# REGISTRY_QUERY_AUTHORITY | ACPI_AUTHORITY, through which acpid acquires the
# tables, the FADT's register blocks, each operation region devmgr grants it
# and each configuration-space access, and pushes each PCI host bridge's
# _OSC grant, windows and interrupt routes (services/acpid/README.md). acpid
# is the only recipe seeding it.
#
# `restart = on_failure`: svcmgr re-creates acpid and re-serves a fresh RECV
# on the persistent endpoint, so pwrmgr's acpid.power SEND survives a
# crash-restart; acpid re-acquires its capabilities from devmgr and runs its
# start-up again; devmgr keeps the results of the first start (and the
# memory regions it granted).
# `critical = no`: devmgr never waits on acpid, so the system runs on
# without it, without the _OSC grant and the routes it pushes, and on x86-64
# without pwrmgr's shutdown and reboot.
binary    = /services/acpid
priority  = 16
restart   = on_failure
critical  = no
namespace = none
provides  = acpid.power:auth acpid.deny:deny
seed      = devmgr.acpi
